# Packed-apworld sources: force LF in every working tree.
#
# scripts/build/pack_apworld.py (and pack_json_tools_installer.py) zip these
# files with `zf.write(path, ...)` — raw bytes straight off the working tree.
# The tracked apworlds/*.apworld artifacts were packed on Linux and are pure
# LF, so on a CRLF checkout (Windows, core.autocrlf=true) a fresh pack differs
# from the tracked artifact in EVERY text entry. That broke
# worlds/json_tools_installer/test/test_packed_apworld_freshness.py on both
# Windows CI jobs, and — worse — meant a Windows contributor running the pack
# script would commit a CRLF artifact that then failed the same test for
# everyone else.
#
# `text=auto` keeps git's binary autodetection (these dirs are all text today:
# json/md/py/txt/csv/dot/yaml); `eol=lf` pins the checkout. The index is
# already LF throughout, so this changes no content — only how CRLF platforms
# materialize it.
worlds/metamath/** text=auto eol=lf
worlds/depgraph/** text=auto eol=lf
worlds/jta/** text=auto eol=lf
worlds/bakingadventure/** text=auto eol=lf
worlds/codingadventure/** text=auto eol=lf
worlds/json_tools_installer/** text=auto eol=lf
worlds/spoiler_export_hook/** text=auto eol=lf

# The spoiler-hook client is vendored byte-for-byte into every world that uses
# it (hand-written worlds and generated packages alike), and
# test/test_spoiler_export_hook.py + test/test_spoiler_hook_callers.py pin each
# copy to worlds/spoiler_export_hook's bytes. One rule for every copy anywhere
# in the tree, so a new copy is covered without a per-world line: without it a
# CRLF checkout (Windows) materializes CRLF for any copy outside the dirs above
# and the byte pin goes red.
**/_spoiler_hook_client.py text eol=lf

# The packed artifacts themselves are zips — never touch them.
apworlds/*.apworld binary
